{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792305977,"nonce":"APT//aoyGOx20ejo3KqQws3BNEg=","title":"Resident heap stays 3-6x the live bytes after a major collection: sparse blocks are neither compacted nor returned","message":"Reported by the ChimpMonk session (their eb19594; probe: `MEMORY_DUMP=\u003cdir\u003e scripts/editor.sh --memory-probe`, which reads hl.Gc.stats() after two hl.Gc.major() calls and writes hl.Gc.dumpMemory with the census from 3f6a867).\n\nNumbers they measured after two majors:\n- Bare UI workspace: 7.9 MB pointer-live + 5.6 MB pointer-free = 13.5 MB live (census), heap (stats().currentMemory) 45.1 MB.\n- 16 panels open: 29.5 + 11.2 = 40.7 MB live, heap 234.7 MB; 240 MB were allocated since the previous stage.\n- After the panels close the heap falls to 60.9 MB, so blocks are released once their objects die.\n\nTheir reading: live objects are spread thinly over many blocks after a burst of short-lived allocation among them, and a major neither compacts nor returns them. Wanted, if feasible: an evacuating pass for sparse blocks, or returning fully empty blocks promptly. For the editor that would take resident memory from about 235 MB to about 80.\n\nTo establish before designing anything:\n- What currentMemory counts (blocks in use, or also free and pending-handback blocks, TLAB blocks, memory not yet returned to the OS).\n- The distribution of live lines per used block in their 16-panel dump (the `# block \u003caddr\u003e \u003clive-lines\u003e \u003clive-bytes\u003e` rows): many blocks with a few lines each is fragmentation; many with none is a release gap.\n- Whether line recycling (5f0aaae) is reusing the free lines of those blocks for the next allocation, in which case the resident figure is the high-water mark and not a leak.\n\nRelated: the earlier block-retention work (1GB heap from fragmentation), and the census in 3f6a867. Their offer: run any ash change through the probe and report numbers.","files":null}]}